{"author":{"id":"2eecc7d99b502c158aacb0868dca9358610edc02724d21fe49b0196402d3364e"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1794425705,"nonce":"j+95ugEuY8rrFIVyfRRLsTkbd2s=","title":"Found 2026-11-12 while checking a workaround the devops-gitops pilot (0.3.2) proposed. Read from the code; nothing run.","message":"ADR-0055 §D says `k8s_overlay` is \"strategic-merged onto the generated Pod\". The code does an RFC-7386 JSON merge-patch: `apply_placement` -\u003e `json_merge` (crates/scarab-executor-k8s/src/lib.rs:5114-5182). Objects merge; every array is replaced whole. The same function merges the baseline and the PlacementProfiles' `k8s:` blocks, so the mismatch reaches all three layers.\n\nWhat breaks:\n\n1. `k8s_overlay` on a container. An overlay such as `{\"spec\":{\"containers\":[{\"name\":\"step\",\"securityContext\":{…}}]}}` replaces the whole `containers` list. The step container loses its image, command, env and mounts, and the Pod is broken or runs something else. Strategic merge would have merged by container `name`. The pilot planned to relax seccomp this way; it does not work.\n\n2. Baseline plus profile tolerations (more likely to hit someone). ADR-0055 §A makes tolerations a baseline fact, stamped on every Pod. A profile that adds its own toleration (an `arm64` profile for a tainted arm64 pool) replaces the baseline's `tolerations` list rather than adding to it, so the baseline toleration is dropped without a word. The same goes for `nodeSelector`? No: that is an object and merges. Lists affected: `tolerations`, `containers`, `initContainers`, `volumes`, `imagePullSecrets`, `topologySpreadConstraints`, affinity terms.\n\nOptions:\nA. Make the code match the ADR: Kubernetes strategic merge for Pod specs (merge keys: containers/initContainers by `name`, volumes by `name`, tolerations appended, etc.). kube-rs has no client-side strategic merge, so either a small merge keyed on the Pod fields Scarab cares about, or a server-side dry-run apply. Check the result against the containers Scarab builds: an overlay must not be able to drop or rename the egress sidecar or the workspace fetcher (ADR-0042/0061), whatever merge is used.\nB. Keep merge-patch and amend ADR-0055 to say so, plus a compile/admission diagnostic when a profile or overlay sets a list the baseline also sets, and docs that say lists replace.\n\nA matches what the ADR promised and what operators expect from Kubernetes. Item 2 should be fixed or diagnosed under either option, since it fails silently.\n\nAcceptance: a test with a baseline toleration and a profile toleration shows both on the Pod (A) or a diagnostic (B); a test with a `k8s_overlay` naming the step container keeps its image, command, env and mounts (A) or is refused (B); ADR-0055 and the code say the same thing.","files":null}]}